  "query_preview": "/Monday through Thursday/... \nWith northern stream jet energy retreating towards the Canadian \nborder under broad ridging, several weak low amplitude features \nwill align in the southern stream over northern Mexico during the \nfirst half of the week before large scale hemispheric pattern \namplification becomes a dominating feature. Predominant anti- \ncyclonic flow will continue to promote surface pressure rises over\nthe intermountain West and dry N/NE boundary layer flow. The vast\nmajority of ensemble membership keeps any notable shortwave \nascent removed from the CWA, and confidence is very good that dry \nconditions will prevail through at least the middle of the week. \nWith H5 heights remaining nearly constant in a 580-584dm range, \nforecast numerical spread is rather narrow indicating very good \nconfidence in a persistence type forecast and readings very near \nclimatology. Ensemble spread grows markedly towards the end of \nnext week as pronounced northern stream pattern amplification \ndriven largely by an unusual early season sudden stratospheric \nwarming (SSW) event unfolds. The perturbation of the polar vortex \nshould cause poleward ridge retrogression while deep negative \nheight anomalies descend equatorward into the NE Conus and Pacific\nNW. While operational models are consistent in focusing strong \njet energy and Pacific moisture towards northern California with \ninconsequential shortwave energy into the Southwest, there is a \nnon- trivial amount of NAEFS members suggesting much deeper, \nintense troughing moving into the local area next weekend. Given \nthis amplification phase, continued wide ensemble spread and \npotentially large variations in operational models yielding low \nforecast confidence should be expected into next week.",
